{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794914844742,"sessionID":"ses_e16bf3244ffe3dp0Kl8aoaMvCb","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_966d8315837e4bde9d84f3e4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Trace currency import bug","prompt":"Explore this Python repository to trace the full path for `python -m ledgerly import ... --format rabo` through storage and `python -m ledgerly report income`. Identify why a Rabobank EUR statement could result in a transaction with amount=None and currency EUR/other, especially around blank balance rows or malformed CSV rows. Also inspect currency conversion and atomic import/error handling requirements. Do not edit files. Return: relevant file paths and line numbers, exact root cause hypothesis, existing tests and conventions, and recommended minimal fix plus regression test cases. Thoroughness: very thorough.","subagent_type":"explore","command":"python -m ledgerly report income 2026-03"},"output":"<task id=\"ses_e16bf2260ffe5CbEF9pZMh3vca\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\n## Conclusion\n\nThere is a confirmed defect in the Rabobank reader: **an unparseable Rabobank amount can become a real `Transaction` whose `amount` is `None`**. The transaction is then categorized, stored in the ledger as JSON `null`, reloaded as `None`, and crashes `report income` during currency conversion.\n\nThe most likely concrete trigger is even more specific: the Rabobank format documentation says positive amounts may be written as `+1.250,00`, but `EURO_AMOUNT` rejects an explicit `+`. Such a valid-looking row therefore parses as `None`.\n\nBlank balance rows are only safe when they are explicitly recognized as balance rows. The current implementation conflates:\n\n1. an empty amount, which may be a balance row, and\n2. a malformed amount, which must be an import error.\n\nThat conflation is the central bug.\n\n---\n\n## Relevant files\n\nAll paths below are absolute.\n\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/__main__.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/io/csv_import.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/io/bank_formats.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/core/transactions.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/core/journal.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/io/json_store.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/rules/categorize.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/fx/convert.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/fx/rates.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/core/money.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/reports/income.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/ledgerly/core/errors.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/README.md`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/tests/test_import.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/tests/test_cli.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/tests/test_rules_fx_reports.py`\n- `/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out2/amend-opencode/ledger-misleading-trace/opencode-1/repo/tests/helpers.py`\n\n---\n\n## Full execution path\n\n### 1. CLI import\n\nIn `ledgerly/__main__.py`:\n\n- Lines 40–85 build the parser.\n- Lines 56–59 define `import FILE --format generic|ing|rabo --account ID`.\n- Lines 88–90 load the existing ledger and rules.\n- Lines 102–109 call `import_statement(...)`.\n- Line 106 sets `changed = result.added > 0`.\n- Lines 148–150 save the ledger only after the import returns successfully.\n\n`main()` at lines 153–158 catches only `LedgerError`. A well-formed `ImportError_` therefore becomes:\n\n```text\nerror: <message>\n```\n\nwith exit status 2. Raw `TypeError` and `ValueError` exceptions are not caught.\n\n### 2. CSV loading and structural validation\n\nIn `ledgerly/io/csv_import.py`:\n\n- `read_statement()` at lines 60–70 reads the whole file and materializes the reader as a list.\n- `rows()` at lines 46–57 uses `csv.DictReader`.\n- Lines 51–54 reject rows with more fields than the header.\n- Lines 55–56 skip wholly empty rows.\n- Line 57 strips header names and converts missing cell values to `\"\"`.\n\nImportant details:\n\n- A row with fewer fields than the header is not rejected there; missing trailing cells become empty strings.\n- `csv.DictReader` uses the standard CSV defaults, not `strict=True`, so malformed quoting is not explicitly guaranteed to be rejected.\n- Because `read_statement()` uses `list(...)`, parsing completes before any transaction is added to the journal. That is the mechanism that gives malformed-row imports their atomic behavior once the reader raises an error.\n\n### 3. Rabobank amount parsing\n\nIn `ledgerly/io/bank_formats.py`:\n\n- Lines 11–13 document the Rabobank format, including `+1.250,00` as a valid signed amount.\n- Line 31 defines:\n\n```python\nEURO_AMOUNT = re.compile(r\"^-?(\\d{1,3}(\\.\\d{3})+|\\d+),\\d{2}$\")\n```\n\nThis accepts an optional minus, but **not an optional plus**.\n\n- `parse_amount()` at lines 34–51 returns `None` when:\n  - the cleaned cell is empty, lines 40–42; or\n  - a decimal-comma amount does not match `EURO_AMOUNT`, lines 43–45.\n\nTherefore both of these produce `None`:\n\n```text\n\"\"       # empty\n\"+1.250,00\"  # documented as valid, but rejected by the regex\n```\n\nOther malformed values such as `12,5`, `EUR 12,50`, `--12,50`, or an unexpected thousands format also produce `None`.\n\n### 4. Rabobank reader\n\nIn the same file:\n\n- Lines 108–109 recognize balance rows only when `Omschrijving-1`, after trimming and lowercasing, **starts with** `\"saldo\"`.\n- `read_rabo()` is at lines 112–126.\n- Lines 114–116 skip a missing amount only if it is a recognized balance row.\n- Lines 117–126 otherwise construct a `Transaction`, including:\n\n```python\namount=amount,  # type: ignore[arg-type]\n```\n\nat line 120.\n\nThat `type: ignore` suppresses the static contradiction with `Transaction.amount: Decimal`.\n\nThe comparison with the other readers makes the omission clear:\n\n- Generic reader, lines 69–71: rejects `None`.\n- ING reader, lines 86–90: rejects `None`.\n- Rabobank reader, lines 114–126: does not reject `None` outside the balance-row branch.\n\nThis is the confirmed control-flow defect.\n\n### 5. Categorization and journal insertion\n\nIn `ledgerly/io/csv_import.py`:\n\n- Line 79 reads the entire statement into a list.\n- Lines 87–91 categorize every transaction.\n- Line 92 calls `journal.extend(...)`.\n\nThe null survives categorization because `ledgerly/rules/categorize.py`:\n\n- Lines 40–43 use `(transaction.amount or 0)` for income/expense-only rules.\n- Lines 80–85 use the same expression for the fallback category.\n\nThus a null amount is treated as zero for categorization. Depending on description and counterparty, it can be assigned to a normal category or to `uncategorised`.\n\nIn `ledgerly/core/journal.py`:\n\n- `add()` at lines 27–41 validates account, closure, category, and duplicate fingerprint, but never validates the amount.\n- The fingerprint in `ledgerly/core/transactions.py:28–42` safely stringifies `None` as `\"None\"`.\n- `extend()` at `journal.py:43–52` therefore accepts the invalid transaction.\n\n### 6. Storage and reload\n\nIn `ledgerly/core/transactions.py`:\n\n- `to_dict()` at lines 54–66 explicitly writes:\n\n```python\n\"amount\": None if self.amount is None else str(self.amount)\n```\n\nso the invalid transaction is persisted as JSON `null`.\n\n- `from_dict()` at lines 68–82 explicitly restores it:\n\n```python\namount=None if raw is None else Decimal(str(raw))\n```\n\nso the corruption survives a ledger reload.\n\nIn `ledgerly/io/json_store.py`:\n\n- Lines 23–41 load the ledger and transactions.\n- Lines 44–60 save it.\n- Lines 52–60 write to a temporary file and use `os.replace`, giving crash-safe file replacement.\n- There is no validation rejecting a null transaction amount.\n\nTherefore the invalid row is not merely transient in memory: it becomes persistent ledger data.\n\n### 7. `report income`\n\nIn `ledgerly/__main__.py`:\n\n- Lines 110–116 load rates and call `income_statement(...)`.\n\nIn `ledgerly/reports/income.py`:\n\n- `category_totals()` at lines 58–67 groups transactions and converts all of them.\n- `income_statement()` at lines 70–86 uses those totals.\n\nIn `ledgerly/fx/convert.py`:\n\n- `convert_all()` at lines 24–25 does not filter invalid transactions.\n- `transaction_in_home()` at lines 19–21 passes `transaction.amount` directly to `to_home()`.\n- `to_home()` at lines 12–16 performs:\n\n```python\nrate = table.rate(currency, day)\nreturn round_cents(amount * rate)\n```\n\nFor a null EUR transaction, `RateTable.rate(\"EUR\", ...)` returns `Decimal(1)`, then `None * Decimal(1)` raises an uncaught `TypeError`.\n\n---\n\n## Exact root-cause hypothesis\n\n### Confirmed root cause\n\n`read_rabo()` treats the `None` returned by `parse_amount()` as a valid transaction amount unless the row is recognized as a balance line. This violates the domain contract that a transaction amount is a `Decimal`.\n\nThe precise failure chain is:\n\n```text\nBedrag=\"+1.250,00\"\n  -> EURO_AMOUNT does not match\n  -> parse_amount() returns None\n  -> row is not recognized as \"Saldo ...\"\n  -> Transaction(amount=None, currency=\"EUR\")\n  -> categorization treats None as 0\n  -> Journal.add() accepts it\n  -> JSON stores \"amount\": null\n  -> report income calls None * Decimal(1)\n  -> TypeError\n```\n\nThe same chain applies to any non-balance row whose amount is malformed or whose blank amount is not recognized as a balance row.\n\n### Likely concrete trigger\n\nThe strongest candidate is an explicit positive sign:\n\n- The module documentation says `+1.250,00` is valid.\n- The regex accepts only an optional minus.\n- The existing test only checks explicit `+` for the decimal-point/generic format, not for the decimal-comma/Rabobank format.\n\nWithout the actual statement, the precise malformed cell cannot be proved, but the code path is exact.\n\n### Blank balance-row nuance\n\nA correctly recognized blank balance row, such as:\n\n```csv\n2026-03-01,,EUR,,,Saldo begin\n```\n\nis skipped today and is covered by an existing test.\n\nHowever:\n\n- A blank ordinary transaction row is not skipped safely; it becomes `None`.\n- A blank balance row whose description does not start with `\"saldo\"` becomes `None`.\n- Labels such as `\"Eindsaldo\"` or `\"Beginsaldo\"` do not satisfy `startswith(\"saldo\")`.\n- Conversely, a genuinely malformed amount on a row whose description starts with `\"Saldo\"` is silently skipped, which can drop money.\n\nThe correct distinction is therefore **empty versus malformed**, not merely `None versus Decimal`.\n\n### Why the currency is EUR or another currency\n\nCurrency is parsed independently at `bank_formats.py:121`:\n\n```python\ncurrency=(row.get(\"Munt\") or \"EUR\").strip().upper()\n```\n\nThus:\n\n- Empty or missing `Munt` becomes `\"EUR\"`.\n- `\"usd\"` becomes `\"USD\"`.\n- `\"other\"` becomes `\"OTHER\"`.\n- A literal `\"EUR/other\"` becomes `\"EUR/OTHER\"`.\n\nThe code does not synthesize a composite `\"EUR/other\"` value. The observed currency is the source `Munt` value, or the EUR default.\n\n`csv_import.py:81–85` only compares transaction currencies with the account currency and emits a warning. It does not validate currencies.\n\n---\n\n## Currency-conversion behavior\n\nIn `ledgerly/fx/rates.py`:\n\n- Lines 43–57 implement rate lookup.\n- EUR is the home currency and has an identity rate, lines 46–48.\n- A foreign rate is the latest rate on or before the transaction date, with a maximum staleness of seven days.\n- A missing rate raises `RateError`, a `LedgerError`.\n- An unknown currency raises `ValueError` from `check_currency()`, not `LedgerError`.\n\nIn `ledgerly/core/money.py`:\n\n- Lines 10–18 restrict currencies to the fixed `KNOWN_CURRENCIES` set.\n- `Money` requires a `Decimal`, lines 32–35.\n\nConsequences for a null transaction:\n\n- **EUR:** identity rate, then `None * Decimal(1)` causes `TypeError`.\n- **Known foreign currency without a rate:** `RateError`, which the CLI handles as status 2.\n- **Known foreign currency with a rate:** eventually the same `TypeError`.\n- **Unknown currency such as `OTHER`:** `ValueError: unknown currency 'OTHER'`, which the CLI does not catch.\n\nThe null amount is therefore not the result of FX conversion; conversion merely exposes the invalid stored transaction.\n\n---\n\n## Atomic import and error-handling requirements\n\n### Intended contract\n\nThe repository explicitly states the intended behavior:\n\n- `README.md:18–19`: an unreadable statement line stops the import with a file-and-line error, and nothing from that file is added.\n- `ledgerly/io/csv_import.py:76`: “all of them, or none on an error.”\n\n### Existing behavior that supports the contract\n\n- `read_statement()` fully materializes the parsed statement before returning.\n- `import_statement()` categorizes all transactions before calling `journal.extend()`.\n- A parser exception therefore occurs before journal mutation.\n- The CLI saves only after `import_statement()` returns successfully.\n- `json_store.save()` writes a temporary file and calls `os.replace()`, so file replacement is atomic.\n\nThus, once `read_rabo()` raises for a malformed amount, valid rows earlier in the same file will not be added, and the on-disk ledger will remain unchanged.\n\n### Gaps and caveats\n\n1. **The current null path never raises**, so atomic error handling is bypassed and invalid data is saved.\n2. `Journal.extend()` is not transactional in memory. If `Journal.add()` rejects a later transaction, earlier transactions may already have been added. For the CLI this normally does not affect the file because the exception prevents saving, but the `import_statement()` docstring overstates the direct API guarantee.\n3. `csv.DictReader` is not configured with `strict=True`; malformed quoting is not explicitly rejected.\n4. Existing ledgers already containing `null` amounts will continue to load them. Fixing only the reader does not repair those ledgers.\n5. `Transaction` has no runtime amount validation, and `Journal.add()` has none either.\n\n---\n\n## Existing tests and conventions\n\n### Existing coverage\n\nIn `tests/test_import.py`:\n\n- Lines 22–26 define the Rabobank fixture.\n- Lines 29–37 test amount parsing.\n- Lines 52–54 test that a recognized blank `\"Saldo begin\"` row is skipped.\n- Lines 56–61 test that generic amount errors name the file and line.\n- Lines 64–71 test categorization and deduplication.\n\nIn `tests/test_cli.py`:\n\n- Lines 23–29 test an ING import followed by an income report.\n\nIn `tests/test_rules_fx_reports.py`:\n\n- Lines 37–45 test rate lookup and direct EUR/USD conversion.\n- Lines 48–70 test income and balance reports.\n\n### Conventions to follow\n\n- Tests use the standard-library `unittest` framework.\n- Test files and fixtures are simple string constants.\n- `tests/helpers.py:34–38` provides `write()` for temporary CSV files.\n- User-correctable parsing failures use `ImportError_`.\n- Error tests use `assertRaisesRegex`, commonly checking the `statement.csv:<line>` prefix.\n- Assertions generally compare exact `Decimal` values and rendered report text.\n- The documented test command is:\n\n```bash\npython -m unittest discover -s tests\n```\n\n### Coverage gaps\n\nThere are currently no tests for:\n\n- an explicit `+` in a decimal-comma amount;\n- a malformed Rabobank amount;\n- a blank non-balance Rabobank amount;\n- malformed CSV structure during a Rabobank import;\n- all-or-none behavior after a failed import;\n- the saved ledger JSON containing no null amounts;\n- an existing null amount being rejected during load;\n- Rabobank foreign-currency import and conversion;\n- CLI exit status 2 and unchanged ledger after a failed import;\n- `json_store` save/load behavior generally.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended minimal fix\n\nThe minimal correctness fix belongs in `ledgerly/io/bank_formats.py`.\n\n### 1. Accept the documented positive sign\n\nChange the Rabobank amount expression conceptually from optional-minus to optional-sign:\n\n```python\nEURO_AMOUNT = re.compile(r\"^[+-]?(\\d{1,3}(\\.\\d{3})+|\\d+),\\d{2}$\")\n```\n\nThis preserves strict Dutch decimal formatting while accepting the documented `+1.250,00`.\n\n### 2. Distinguish an empty balance amount from a malformed amount\n\nIn `read_rabo()`:\n\n```python\nraw = _field(row, \"Bedrag\", source, line)\n\nif not raw.strip():\n    if _is_balance_line(row):\n        continue\n    raise ImportError_(\n        f\"cannot read the amount {raw!r}\", source=source, line=line\n    )\n\namount = parse_amount(raw, decimal_comma=True)\nif amount is None:\n    raise ImportError_(\n        f\"cannot read the amount {raw!r}\", source=source, line=line\n    )\n```\n\nThis gives the intended semantics:\n\n- Empty amount plus recognized balance row: skip.\n- Empty amount on an ordinary row: line-specific import error.\n- Malformed amount on any row: line-specific import error.\n- No `Transaction` can be created with `amount=None`.\n- The existing `# type: ignore[arg-type]` can then be removed.\n\nDo **not** replace this with `if amount is None: continue`: that would silently drop ordinary malformed transactions and violate the README’s “never drops or invents money” requirement.\n\nIf actual Rabobank balance labels are broader than `\"Saldo ...\"`, update `_is_balance_line()` using explicit known labels or a narrowly tested pattern. A blank amount without a reliable balance marker should be rejected rather than silently discarded.\n\n### Optional, separate hardening\n\nThese are useful but not required for the minimal reader fix:\n\n- Enforce `Transaction.amount` as a non-null `Decimal` in the domain model.\n- Reject null amounts while loading old ledgers with a clear `LedgerError`.\n- Validate transaction currencies at import and convert unknown-currency failures into `LedgerError`.\n- Consider `strict=True` for CSV parsing.\n- Make `Journal.extend()` truly all-or-none by validating/staging before mutation.\n- Guard `transaction_in_home()` so an already-corrupt ledger produces a clean ledger error rather than a raw `TypeError`.\n\n---\n\n## Recommended regression tests\n\n### In `tests/test_import.py`\n\n1. **Explicit positive decimal-comma sign**\n   - Input: `\"+1.234,56\"`.\n   - Expected: `Decimal(\"1234.56\")`.\n\n2. **Recognized blank balance remains skipped**\n   - Existing `\"Saldo begin\"` case.\n   - Also test case/whitespace variation if that is intended.\n\n3. **Malformed non-balance Rabobank amount**\n   - For example `\"12,5\"`.\n   - Expected: `ImportError_` matching `statement.csv:<line>: cannot read the amount`.\n\n4. **Blank non-balance Rabobank amount**\n   - Expected: the same line-specific `ImportError_`.\n   - No transaction should be returned.\n\n5. **Malformed amount on a saldo-described row**\n   - Expected: error, not silent skipping.\n   - This locks in the distinction between blank balance rows and malformed rows.\n\n6. **Failed import is atomic**\n   - Put a valid row before a malformed row.\n   - Call `import_statement()`.\n   - Expect `ImportError_`.\n   - Assert the journal still has zero transactions.\n\n7. **No null Rabobank transactions**\n   - Import a valid Rabobank file.\n   - Assert every returned transaction has a `Decimal` amount.\n\n8. **Structural malformed CSV**\n   - Add a row with an extra column.\n   - Expect the existing “more field(s) than the header” `ImportError_`.\n   - Assert no journal mutation.\n\n### In `tests/test_cli.py`\n\n9. **Rabobank import followed by income report**\n   - Import the Rabobank fixture.\n   - Run `report income`.\n   - Assert the expected total and category text.\n   - Inspect the saved JSON and assert no transaction has `\"amount\": null`.\n\n10. **Malformed Rabobank import exits cleanly**\n    - Exercise `main()` rather than only `run()`.\n    - Assert return code 2.\n    - Assert stderr starts with `error:` and names the source line.\n    - Assert the ledger file is unchanged or remains absent.\n\n11. **Foreign currency path**\n    - Import a valid USD Rabobank row.\n    - Supply a USD rate and verify conversion in the report.\n    - Separately verify a missing rate produces `RateError`/status 2 rather than a `TypeError`.\n\n12. **Existing corrupt ledger**\n    - If load-time validation is added, load JSON containing `\"amount\": null`.\n    - Assert a clear `LedgerError` identifying the ledger/transaction rather than a later report-time `TypeError`.\n\nThe most important regression assertion is not merely that malformed rows raise. It is that **a failed Rabobank import leaves both the in-memory journal and the on-disk ledger unchanged**, while every persisted transaction has a real `Decimal` amount.\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_e16bf3244ffe3dp0Kl8aoaMvCb","sessionId":"ses_e16bf2260ffe5CbEF9pZMh3vca","model":{"modelID":"minimax/minimax-m3.1-flash","providerID":"openrouter"},"truncated":false},"title":"Trace currency import bug","time":{"start":1794914704805,"end":1794914844727}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[]}},"id":"prt_1e940dd99001CJ1d7XhbqEUz8R","sessionID":"ses_e16bf3244ffe3dp0Kl8aoaMvCb","messageID":"msg_1e940d702001RA9wuazl3Ojsjj"}}
